I thought I'd move this YANI to its own thread since it struck me as
actually being quite good, and I think it got lost among all the
discussions of Jabberwocky over in the main thread. (I was actually
really hoping somebody with an itch for writing patches might want to
write it up, since I entirely lack the expertise to do so. Where's L
gotten to?)

YANI: Lump of salt
Classification: a rock/gemstone. However, one can both (e)at and
(a)pply it as well.
Uses: It would be useful for a wide variety of things (see below) all
of which would of course use up the lump of salt:
- (a)pplying to a corpse would un-rot it (re-set its counter to zero)
- Throwing it at zombies (and scoring a successful hit) would make them

instantly disintegrate.
- Throwing it at any vegetative or slimy creature (worms, molds, fungi,

puddings, and so forth) would inflict massive damage, and cause them to

"shriek in pain."
- Eating it would produce an appropriate message, like, "Blah! You
cough and spit."
- However, dogs, cats, horses, and any other salt-loving pet would
consider it a treat (like tripe rations or meatballs).
- Applying it to a potion of water would turn it into salt water,
capable of curing sickness but also causing hallucination (credit to
Robert Shaw for this one, see original thread)
- Dropping it on an ice square would dissolve the ice.
- Throwing it at freshwater creatures would inflict damage (by turning
their water to salt) (again, thanks to Robert Shaw for this one, and
the next)
- another possible use is to combine it with a potion of acid and then
dip various comestibles in (corpses? vegetables?) to pickle them.
However, I'm not sure how valuable this would be. Allowing indefinite
preservation of all corpses is too powerful (i.e. cockatrice corpses.)
Maybe only vegetative corpses (e.g. fungi and molds)?

Other attributes:
- Levitating and dropping it on the floor (or dropping it down stairs,
a hole, etc.) would use up the salt and be -1 to luck ("You spill the
salt all over the floor! You feel unlucky.")
- Salt would be worth a great deal in shops, since in medieval times it

was a highly valued substance.
- It would have a much greater chance of being found in the Mines. As
well, there could be an special, rarely-occurring room (like a treasure

zoo) containing big lumps of salt hanging from the ceiling: "You enter
a salt cave!" Of course, among the salt would be various piercers,
disguised as stalactites, waiting to drop on your unsuspecting head.
This would be a new species of piercer - salt piercer - and would do
extra damage by rubbing salt into the wounds they create. (Again,
thanks to Robert Shaw for this idea.)

Thoughts?

